Title: Duck typing on anonymous object literals and class objects
Description: Based on new duck typing concept fresh objects can not have access property.
Like this

``` javascript
let a = { name:"Bill"};
let b = {name:"Bill", age:20};

a = {name: "James", age:30 }; // Error - excess property not allowed
a = b; // No error - works fine
```

Now the questions is does this concept also applies to class objects
Like this:

``` javascript
class Human {    
    name: string;    
    constructor(name: string){
        this.name = name;
    }
}

class Animal {
    name: string;
    age: number;    
    constructor(name: string, age: number){
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
    eat(){
        console.log(this.name + " is a Human and is eating");
    }
}

let h1 = new Human("James");
let a1 = new Animal("Dog", 2);
h1 = new Animal("Cat", 1); //this is working -- But based on duck typing concept it should not because it has excess property and its fresh object
```

The error is same if I remove constructor.

Is there any difference in duck typing concept for anonymous objects and class objects OR may be I'm missing something?
Or is it really considered as fresh object?


